`EnsureInitialized()` is the only call that starts the part. Every other call returns `Error::NotInitialized`
until it has succeeded, and no read ever starts the part on its own.

## Driver coverage

<!-- generated: chip-coverage -->
<!-- /generated -->

This driver powers it up, checks its power-on self-test pulse, reads OUT several times so a noisy or floating line is an
error rather than a wrong answer, and puts it to sleep. It runs on any MCU: you give it a small class that reads one pin
(and optionally drives another), and it does the rest.

| | |
| --- | --- |
| **Does** | Asserts OUT (open drain, active low) above its trip point, 85 °C typical |
| **Bus** | None: GPIO only, OUT to an MCU input |
| **Also drives** | SHDN_N, when your port has `GpioSet()`; then `EnsureInitialized()` checks the self-test pulse |
| **Driver** | Header-only C++20; no heap, exceptions, RTTI or virtual calls; every call returns `Result<T>` |
| **Runs on** | Any MCU with GPIO and a C++20 compiler; the host example runs on any PC ([Platforms](docs/platforms.md)) |
| **Status** | 0.1.0, not yet verified on hardware: host-tested against a model of the pins |

- Talks to the part through GPIO only: no data bus.
- Power-cycles the part (SHDN_N, when your port drives it) and checks its self-test pulse on OUT.
- Reads OUT several times per reading and reports a floating line instead of a wrong answer.
- Returns `Result<T>` with an `Error` from every call: no exceptions, no heap, no RTTI, no virtual calls.

